WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:07.003
"Grandpa, you're home!"

2
00:00:18.189 --> 00:00:20.250
cried, running into Grandpa's study.

3
00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.752
"How was Italy?"

4
00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:26.834
"Laurie! It was wonderful!

5
00:00:27.574 --> 00:00:30.536
I wish you could have gone too," Grandpa said.

6
00:00:31.444 --> 00:00:36.446
"I studied many artifacts from ancient Rome that I had never seen before."

7
00:00:36.466 --> 00:00:39.147
"Did you bring anything back to show me?

8
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.128
A toga?"

9
00:00:40.528 --> 00:00:41.328
Laurie asked.

10
00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.250
Grandpa laughed.

11
00:00:47.751 --> 00:00:50.852
"Unfortunately I couldn't bring home any togas.

12
00:00:51.773 --> 00:00:54.474
But I did put together a slide show!"

13
00:00:55.935 --> 00:00:59.136
Grandpa switched off the lights and turned on the projector.

14
00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:06.425
"Ooh, is that Julius Caesar?"

15
00:01:07.086 --> 00:01:09.908
Laurie asked, looking at a picture of a statue.

16
00:01:09.928 --> 00:01:12.430
"No, but you're close!

17
00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:20.355
This is Octavian, Caesar's grandnephew, who came to power after Caesar was murdered.

18
00:01:21.416 --> 00:01:26.099
He's more commonly known as Augustus, the first emperor of Rome."

19
00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:31.743
"Wait—if he was the first emperor, who was Julius Caesar?"

20
00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:32.964
Laurie asked.

21
00:01:34.104 --> 00:01:37.546
"Hmm Maybe we should start from the beginning.

22
00:01:39.047 --> 00:01:46.831
According to legend, long ago two brothers named Romulus and Remus were left in a basket on the

23
00:01:46.911 --> 00:01:49.012
Tiber River in present-day Italy.

24
00:01:50.253 --> 00:01:53.855
A wolf saved the baby boys and fed them.

25
00:01:55.055 --> 00:02:00.118
Then a shepherd and his wife found the boys and raised them as their own.

26
00:02:01.158 --> 00:02:03.820
The two brothers grew up to be brave and strong.

27
00:02:05.068 --> 00:02:07.869
One day they decided to build a city.

28
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.990
But they got into a fight.

29
00:02:11.211 --> 00:02:18.114
Romulus killed his brother, named the city 'Rome' after himself, and became the first king.

30
00:02:19.214 --> 00:02:22.135
This was in 753 BC.

31
00:02:23.296 --> 00:02:28.838
A series of kings continued to rule Rome for the next 250 years or so.

32
00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:36.682
But eventually Romans grew to dislike kings, because they thought kings gave themselves too much power.

33
00:02:37.843 --> 00:02:44.907
Instead they formed a government run by the people, or the public, so Rome became a republic.

34
00:02:46.047 --> 00:02:49.269
Julius Caesar was the last leader of the republic.

35
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:56.954
When that system collapsed, Augustus began a new style of rule and became the first emperor of the

36
00:02:57.034 --> 00:02:58.655
new Roman Empire.

37
00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:06.640
Augustus was a great commander who led Rome into an age of cultural and geographical expansion.

38
00:03:07.761 --> 00:03:12.404
The name of the month August comes from his name, which means the great one.

39
00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:18.048
People liked him because during his rule Roman civil wars finally ended.

40
00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:24.332
A long period of peace began, and the Roman Empire grew into the most powerful empire in the

41
00:03:24.372 --> 00:03:32.770
world. It included not only modern-day Italy, but England, France, Germany, Egypt, and Turkey as well."

42
00:03:34.191 --> 00:03:35.312
A new slide appeared.

43
00:03:35.332 --> 00:03:39.935
"Ha-ha! Grandpa, is that you in a scuba suit?"

44
00:03:40.355 --> 00:03:41.056
Laurie said.

45
00:03:41.076 --> 00:03:42.617
"It is!

46
00:03:43.237 --> 00:03:44.959
I was diving for artifacts.

47
00:03:45.939 --> 00:03:48.781
Many Roman remains have been found underwater!

48
00:03:49.642 --> 00:03:52.664
This is because the Romans traded mainly by sea.

49
00:03:53.564 --> 00:03:59.456
They built great ships and sailed from ports near Rome to other cities in Europe, as well as

50
00:03:59.496 --> 00:04:00.957
to Africa and the Middle East.

51
00:04:02.278 --> 00:04:04.319
But not all journeys were successful.

52
00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:12.003
Sunken ships full of trade items have become valuable resources for people studying ancient Rome.

53
00:04:13.104 --> 00:04:16.986
For example, these pots were discovered aboard a sunken ship.

54
00:04:18.046 --> 00:04:22.889
We now know they were used to carry olive oil, sauces, and wine for trade."

55
00:04:22.909 --> 00:04:27.171
"Hey, what's that?"

56
00:04:27.691 --> 00:04:29.948
Laurie asked, pointing at the next slide.

57
00:04:30.949 --> 00:04:33.129
It looked like a picture of an old pool.

58
00:04:33.689 --> 00:04:34.530
"It's pretty."

59
00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:38.171
"Those are the ruins of a bathhouse.

60
00:04:39.411 --> 00:04:42.732
Bathhouses were cheap and very popular in ancient Rome.

61
00:04:43.432 --> 00:04:44.793
Nearly everyone went.

62
00:04:45.673 --> 00:04:54.215
Just like today's spas, Roman bathhouses had hot tubs, steam rooms, saunas, and cold pools to plunge into

63
00:04:54.276 --> 00:04:55.656
before or after your bath!

64
00:04:56.972 --> 00:04:59.114
The bathhouse wasn't just somewhere to bathe.

65
00:05:00.256 --> 00:05:03.719
It was also a place for families and friends to go and relax.

66
00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:09.565
Another popular place in Roman cities was the forum.

67
00:05:10.606 --> 00:05:13.409
It was like the downtown area in a modern city.

68
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:20.016
Besides being a center for politics, the forum was usually a major shopping center!

69
00:05:21.016 --> 00:05:27.819
There you could walk along rows of merchants and craftsmen selling pottery, jewelry, spices, and wine.

70
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.163
And if you got hungry, you could purchase some fast food— maybe olives, beans, and bread—from a food

71
00:05:35.223 --> 00:05:40.605
stand. These are the walls of a market near the forum in ancient Rome.

72
00:05:41.786 --> 00:05:46.588
The Romans were great builders, so many remains are still in good condition.

73
00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:54.224
But even though we'll never be able to see exactly how the Romans lived, we hear the ancient

74
00:05:54.284 --> 00:05:57.206
Romans every day—in our languages!

75
00:05:58.446 --> 00:06:01.908
Their language, Latin, isn't spoken much anymore.

76
00:06:03.009 --> 00:06:06.331
But it is the root language for many European languages.

77
00:06:07.612 --> 00:06:11.374
We call them Romance languages because of their connection to Rome.

78
00:06:12.574 --> 00:06:15.156
Many English words come from Latin as well.

79
00:06:16.256 --> 00:06:21.939
So even if you don't know Latin, if you know English or a Romance language, you can probably

80
00:06:22.079 --> 00:06:23.780
figure out what many words mean.

81
00:06:24.901 --> 00:06:30.764
For example, our word 'history' comes from the Latin word historia!

82
00:06:32.385 --> 00:06:36.267
Our language owes a lot to Roman gods and goddesses too.

83
00:06:37.227 --> 00:06:43.991
Romans thought the gods lived near the heavens, and they believed the movements of the stars were gods

84
00:06:44.391 --> 00:06:45.892
traveling across the sky.

85
00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.762
This is why many of our planets are named after gods and goddesses!

86
00:06:51.562 --> 00:06:57.164
Jupiter, Mars, Mercury, Neptune, and Saturn were all mighty gods.

87
00:06:58.105 --> 00:07:00.886
And Venus was the goddess of love and beauty."

88
00:07:02.266 --> 00:07:06.488
"Laurie, we have to leave," Laurie heard Dad call from downstairs.

89
00:07:06.508 --> 00:07:08.929
"You'll be late for your violin lesson!"

90
00:07:08.949 --> 00:07:11.790
"Uh-oh. Time to go.

91
00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.071
Thanks, Grandpa.

92
00:07:13.451 --> 00:07:16.232
Those were interesting photos," Laurie said.

93
00:07:19.368 --> 00:07:23.151
"Wait it's not a toga, but I have something for you."

94
00:07:24.792 --> 00:07:26.873
Grandpa pulled a ring from his pocket.

95
00:07:26.893 --> 00:07:29.835
"A ring with your initials on it.

96
00:07:30.836 --> 00:07:38.761
And 2010 in Roman numerals on the inside," he said, "so you'll always remember when I gave it

97
00:07:38.801 --> 00:07:39.122
to you."

98
00:07:39.142 --> 00:07:41.363
"Oh, thank you!

99
00:07:41.783 --> 00:07:42.504
I love it!"

100
00:07:43.004 --> 00:07:45.726
Laurie put the ring on and gave Grandpa a hug.

101
00:07:45.746 --> 00:07:48.148
"Now, go!

102
00:07:48.608 --> 00:07:49.871
Don't keep your dad waiting."

103
00:07:50.413 --> 00:07:51.114
He smiled.

104
00:07:51.134 --> 00:07:53.259
"Bye, Grandpa!"
